    {
      "by": "greptile-apps",
      "association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "at": "2026-09-28T08:42:43Z",
      "excerpt": "That’s a fair point. These headers are protocol literals used directly at the JS/Python boundary, and the surrounding `Reflex-Client-Token`/`Reflex-Event-Handler` headers follow the same convention; introducing a JS-only constant would not improve consistency or enable sharing. I’m withdrawing this comment—no change is needed here."
    },
    {
      "by": "greptile-apps",
      "association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "at": "2026-10-17T16:49:58Z",
      "excerpt": "<a href=\"#\"><img alt=\"P1\" src=\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/p1.svg?v=9\" align=\"top\"></a> **Buttons run the wrong handler**\n\nAn unmapped handler can collide with a minified handler. With handlers `foo` and `a` and an events map containing only `foo: \"a\"`, both resolve to the same wire name. Registering the second handler overwrites the first, so clicking one button can run the other handler.\n\nThis can happen when a handler is added before `sync` runs. Check resolved name"
    },
    {
      "by": "greptile-apps",
      "association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "at": "2026-10-17T16:49:59Z",
      "excerpt": "<a href=\"#\"><img alt=\"P1\" src=\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/p1.svg?v=9\" align=\"top\"></a> **Deleted states return after reload**\n\n`refresh_keys` brings back states removed by `reload_state_module`. Reload removes classes from the parent's substate set but leaves them in `base_states`. This new loop rebuilds that set from every entry in `base_states`.\n\nIf a reload deletes or renames a state, installing a resolver afterward puts the old class back into the tree. That bring"
    },
    {
      "by": "benedikt-bartscher",
      "association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "at": "2026-10-17T17:32:07Z",
      "excerpt": "Valid. The collision comes from #6100's handler naming, so it is fixed there in 55bbc45d78 and synced here in 8cdbb67079. Registering a handler now raises `StateValueError` if another handler of the same state already resolves to that name. The message names both handlers and points at `reflex minify sync`. A reloaded class can still replace its old entries. The `reflex minify` commands run with default names, so this cannot block `sync`. Regression test: `test_handlers_resolving_to_one_name_rai"
    },
    {
      "by": "benedikt-bartscher",
      "association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "at": "2026-10-17T17:32:09Z",
      "excerpt": "Valid. `refresh_keys` comes from #6100, so it is fixed there in 55bbc45d78 and synced here in 8cdbb67079. It now re-keys the current substate sets instead of rebuilding them from `base_states`, so a state a reload removed stays removed. Regression test: `test_refresh_keys_keeps_reloaded_substates_out`."
    }
  ],
